think The r4 exploration named the market/accounting/interop modules (mandi · greencandy · glow/nock) plus the tools/*.rish prose as the closing work. glow/nock is delicate (a phased-plan structure and a print string a witness may assert) and the tools prose is its own set, so I scoped this round to mandi + greencandy (17 sites) and left glow/nock + tools + the VOLS-close re-run for the next rounds — one keystone, one send.
think mandi: four .rye version headers (Mandi lap 1/lap 3) named by capability (market listing · listing settle) + git stamp 20260728; the README status line and four section headings (## Lap 1, ## Floor view (lap 2), …) named by feature. greencandy: two lap-2/lap-4 feature comments named by report + stamp 20260713; the README status/design/link labels named by report — the greencandy_sight_view_lap3.rish tool handle kept (a filename, prose-only ruling), the dated design-doc filenames (…_greencandy-lap2-reports.md) left untouched (dated testimony, and no space so the guard never bit them anyway).
think A real trap surfaced and was caught: a `.`-for-middot regex byte matched only one of the two UTF-8 bytes of · (0xC2 0xB7), orphaning a byte on greencandy/README.md line 60 (broke grep's UTF-8, and greencandy_core line 5 silently missed its stamp). Reverted that README with git checkout, redid the link-label edits with byte-exact \xc2\xb7 patterns, added the missing stamp, and verified every edited file with iconv -f utf-8 -t utf-8 — all valid UTF-8, residual 0.
obs Comment-and-README only; proven safe on metal — mandi_listing_settle + mandi_lap1 (build mandi_core/listing_settle_core + selftests) and greencandy_lap4 + gism_ayre_j8 (build greencandy_core + greencandy.rye) all GREEN.
